<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #eaf2f8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Indigenous Heritage</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Learning About Our Diverse Cultures</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Summarize articles for classroom discussions. Concept: Explain cultural differences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain cultural differences by using pictures of traditional artifacts and gestures (e.g., points to a handmade Ngäbe-Buglé chácara and explains how it is woven by hand, holds up a Guna Yala mola and describes its vibrant patterns and stories, or shows an Emberá basket and explains how it is crafted from natural fibers like palm leaves).
                Can explain Indigenous cultural differences using visuals and gestures (e.g., shows a picture of traditional clothing and gestures to highlight its colors).
                Can summarize key points from an article about indigenous cultures to help peers understand important traditions and practices during classroom discussions (e.g., creating a mind map about how the Naso Tjër Di celebrate their festival).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> "Cultural Artifact Guessing Game" – The teacher displays images of various Panamanian indigenous artifacts (e.g., a Ngäbe-Buglé chácara, a Guna Yala mola, an Emberá basket, a Naso Tjër Di flute). Students, in pairs, briefly discuss what they remember about the object and the culture it belongs to. The teacher encourages them to use vocabulary like "traditional," "colorful," "handmade," and verbs like "preserve" and "celebrate."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to briefly explain one artifact, focusing on summarization and key details. For example, pointing to a mola, "This is a Guna Yala mola. It is a vibrant, handmade textile. Guna women create these patterns, which tell stories about their ancestral beliefs and history. They have preserved this art form for centuries." The teacher emphasizes stress on words like "vibrant," "ancestral," and "preserved."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the task and key terms.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I explain the mola, do I read a long text, or do I summarize the main ideas?" (Expected answer: Summarize).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'vibrant' mean when we talk about colors? Does it mean dull or bright?" (Expected answer: Bright).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Indigenous Heritage".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short informational text excerpts (digital format) about different aspects of Panamanian indigenous cultures, focusing on traditions and history that students might include in their "Indigenous Culture Website" project. Each text is 2-3 paragraphs. For example, one text about "Naso Tjër Di Harvest Festivals," another on "Emberá Artisanal Craftsmanship," and a third on "Guna Migration and Identity." Genre: Informational texts, short reports. Register: Factual, descriptive. Format: Digital text, short paragraphs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Summarize articles for classroom discussions. Explain cultural differences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read one text individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main tradition or historical event described in the text?" (e.g., "The Naso Tjër Di celebrate their harvest with a special ceremony.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> "Match the text with the correct Indigenous group and its main practice/event." (e.g., Text A: Naso Tjër Di, harvest festival; Text B: Emberá, basket weaving; Text C: Guna, migration).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present simple and past simple for describing Indigenous cultures (e.g., "There are eight Indigenous groups." "Gunas migrated in the 16th century."), adjectives for adding detail (e.g., "colorful traditional clothing"), present perfect for ongoing actions (e.g., "They have preserved traditions.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Indigenous, cultures, traditions, patterns, diversity, migration, heritage, preservation, history, customs, ancestry, community, language, identity, art, knowledge, vibrant, colorful, preserved, traditional, diverse, ancestral, cultural, historical. Verbs: preserve, respect, celebrate, migrate, honor.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Focus on stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., "Indigenous," "ancestry," "migration," "identity," "vibrant," and "handmade").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Acknowledging cultural value ("The Guna people's customs are unique and valuable to our nation."), sharing historical importance ("Groups like the Emberá have lived here for centuries and maintain traditional ways.").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Summarize a Headline"</b> (5 min)
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides short headlines related to Indigenous cultures (e.g., "Ngäbe-Buglé Preserve Ancient Language," "Wounaan Art Reflects Nature").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students, individually, write one sentence to summarize the main idea, using a present simple verb and at least one adjective. (e.g., "The Ngäbe-Buglé people actively preserve their ancestral language.")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Peer Explanation Chain"</b> (6 min)
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. The teacher gives each pair a different short paragraph (from Stage 2) about an indigenous tradition or historical fact.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A reads the paragraph silently. Student B then asks Student A to explain the key points in their own words, using the target vocabulary and grammar structures. Student A rephrases and summarizes.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They switch roles with another paragraph. Teacher circulates, listening for pronunciation of multisyllabic words and correct grammar.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Check: "Explain the Visual"</b> (6 min)
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides pairs with a picture of a specific cultural item (e.g., a hand-woven chácara bag, traditional Emberá dress, a Guna shaman's ritual item) without text.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns explaining the item to their partner, describing its features, cultural significance, and connection to the Indigenous group, using gestures and target vocabulary. They should try to incorporate pragmatic functions like "This is valuable to their identity." Their partner provides feedback on clarity and use of language.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">17-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Indigenous Heritage".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format: "Indigenous Culture Website Presentation - Explain Your Section"</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in their existing "Indigenous Culture Website" project groups. Each student in the group is responsible for explaining a specific section or page of their website (e.g., "Naso Tjër Di Traditions," "Emberá Art and Crafts," "Guna Yala History and Migration").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student takes turns acting as an 'expert' for their section. They explain the content of their website page to their group members, summarizing the key information, describing the cultural aspects, and highlighting important traditions or historical facts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students must use visuals (pictures from their website project, mock-ups of artifacts, gestures) to clarify and explain cultural differences and concepts. They should integrate present simple (for current practices), past simple (for historical context like migration), adjectives (vibrant, ancestral), and present perfect (for ongoing preservation: "They have preserved their customs").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They are encouraged to use pragmatic functions: "The Guna people's customs are unique and valuable to our nation," and sociolinguistic functions: "Communities like the Emberá have worked to preserve their art in recent years through community projects."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, observing fluency, collaborative interaction, and the effective mediation of information.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy (observation checklist, rubric, or peer assessment).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student briefly presents a 1-minute summary and explanation of a key aspect of their website section to another group or the whole class (depending on time). They should aim to explain one tradition or historical fact clearly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist / Peer Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarizes key points clearly and concisely.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses visuals and gestures effectively to explain cultural information.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Incorporates target vocabulary (e.g., Indigenous, traditions, vibrant, preserve, heritage).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates correct use of present simple, past simple, and adjectives.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation is intelligible, with appropriate stress on multisyllabic words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Acknowledges cultural value (e.g., "This custom is valuable/important").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of explaining your website section to your classmates today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using pictures or gestures help you explain the cultural information more clearly?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new facts about Panama's Indigenous cultures did you learn or clarify today, and how will you apply this understanding to improve your website project?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel explaining complex cultural information to others using English after today's lesson, especially with visuals and gestures?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did students effectively mediate information by summarizing and explaining cultural differences during their website presentations? Were they able to use visuals and gestures effectively to enhance understanding?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments are needed for future mediation tasks, especially concerning complex vocabulary or historical timelines? Did the integration of the project work well for this mediation lesson?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes our theme on "Learning About Our Diverse Cultures" and the "Indigenous Culture Website" project. Students have now practiced all four skills and mediation to prepare and present their work. Next, we will transition to a new theme, applying our developed language skills to different contexts and content areas.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-4 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>